1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,260
Character is complex.

2
00:00:02,260 --> 00:00:04,400
And I would always challenge my students.

3
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:07,640
I would say, push them to the wall.

4
00:00:07,640 --> 00:00:09,160
Push them to the wall.

5
00:00:09,160 --> 00:00:11,340
And that's where the drama of being alive is.

6
00:00:11,340 --> 00:00:13,520
Can you live with yourself if you

7
00:00:13,520 --> 00:00:16,880
have to do something that you ordinarily would hate to do?

8
00:00:16,880 --> 00:00:19,640
But the circumstance makes it necessary for you.

9
00:00:19,640 --> 00:00:21,800
So you got person against themselves,

10
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:23,280
person against person.

11
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:24,080
Is that it?

12
00:00:24,080 --> 00:00:25,280
No.

13
00:00:25,280 --> 00:00:27,320
There's a third level of drama.

14
00:00:27,320 --> 00:00:28,920
Tell me about these relationships

15
00:00:28,920 --> 00:00:29,940
with characters.

16
00:00:29,940 --> 00:00:34,420
So if character drives drama and voice drives character,

17
00:00:34,420 --> 00:00:36,400
what drives voice?

18
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:39,200
And this might be lesson number one of craft.

19
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:40,780
So when you're writing, what are you

20
00:00:40,780 --> 00:00:45,400
doing to make it feel alive?

21
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:47,200
Read the first sentence again, because you'll

22
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:49,100
see a little registral trick there.

23
00:00:49,100 --> 00:00:50,480
Tell me when to stop.

24
00:00:50,480 --> 00:00:52,780
Each child's tree has its own excellence.

25
00:00:52,780 --> 00:00:55,520
Stop.

26
00:00:55,520 --> 00:00:57,320
Richard Powers won the Pulitzer Prize

27
00:00:57,320 --> 00:00:58,900
for The Overstory in 2000.

28
00:00:58,920 --> 00:01:02,280
And next year, his place runs for four worldwide.

29
00:01:08,280 --> 00:01:09,480
Or the Bestclockwise.

30
00:01:09,480 --> 00:01:10,700
By the way, can you believe it?

31
00:01:10,700 --> 00:01:11,620
I raised my hand.

32
00:01:11,620 --> 00:01:16,840
My vaiila.

33
00:01:16,840 --> 00:01:21,120
I got a little bit redirected.

34
00:01:21,120 --> 00:01:23,760
But the thing about reading narrative is that it's

35
00:01:23,760 --> 00:01:25,140
about showing that you're not alone,

36
00:01:25,140 --> 00:01:27,220
and you're actually moving forward.

37
00:01:27,220 --> 00:01:28,680
And we learned this from our work.

38
00:01:28,680 --> 00:01:28,920
Hi.

39
00:01:28,920 --> 00:01:33,920
I want to show you how drama, conflict, voice, and dialogue

40
00:01:33,920 --> 00:01:35,920
can bring a character to life.

41
00:01:35,920 --> 00:01:37,920
So if you're somebody who's trying to write better stories

42
00:01:37,920 --> 00:01:40,920
and write with more life in your own work,

43
00:01:40,920 --> 00:01:42,920
well, you're going to love this conversation

44
00:01:42,920 --> 00:01:45,920
with Richard Powers.

45
00:01:45,920 --> 00:01:47,920
Character is complex,

46
00:01:47,920 --> 00:01:52,920
and we all do this in the course of our lives.

47
00:01:52,920 --> 00:01:57,920
You know, our brains have adapted

48
00:01:57,920 --> 00:02:02,920
to try to understand the hidden motivations of other people.

49
00:02:02,920 --> 00:02:06,920
In fact, when you talk to evolutionary biologists,

50
00:02:06,920 --> 00:02:08,920
there will be some who say,

51
00:02:08,920 --> 00:02:12,920
we needed the big brain because we were social.

52
00:02:12,920 --> 00:02:14,920
You know, we had...

53
00:02:14,920 --> 00:02:17,920
I mean, mammals have, by and large,

54
00:02:17,920 --> 00:02:20,920
solved a lot of the problems of predation,

55
00:02:20,920 --> 00:02:23,920
of avoiding prey, you know,

56
00:02:23,920 --> 00:02:26,920
responding flexibly to change.

57
00:02:26,920 --> 00:02:27,920
You know,

58
00:02:27,920 --> 00:02:30,920
you can get by on a lot less hardware,

59
00:02:30,920 --> 00:02:32,920
but what you need a lot of hardware for

60
00:02:32,920 --> 00:02:34,920
is keeping track of who's up and who's down,

61
00:02:34,920 --> 00:02:35,920
who's in and who's out,

62
00:02:35,920 --> 00:02:36,920
who owes a favor.

63
00:02:36,920 --> 00:02:37,920
All the interactions.

64
00:02:37,920 --> 00:02:38,920
That's right. That's right.

65
00:02:38,920 --> 00:02:41,920
So we're all novelists in our own lives.

66
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:43,920
We're all saying,

67
00:02:43,920 --> 00:02:44,920
I...

68
00:02:44,920 --> 00:02:46,920
This guy is remembering

69
00:02:46,920 --> 00:02:49,920
what happened between us

70
00:02:49,920 --> 00:02:50,920
20 years ago.

71
00:02:50,920 --> 00:02:51,920
Right.

72
00:02:51,920 --> 00:02:52,920
And he's holding a grudge,

73
00:02:52,920 --> 00:02:55,920
or I haven't seen her in a long time.

74
00:02:55,920 --> 00:02:56,920
I wonder if she's...

75
00:02:56,920 --> 00:02:59,920
I wonder if she's also a little bit nostalgic

76
00:02:59,920 --> 00:03:01,920
about the road that we did not take.

77
00:03:01,920 --> 00:03:03,920
You know, all those kinds of things

78
00:03:03,920 --> 00:03:05,920
that we do with each other all the time.

79
00:03:05,920 --> 00:03:07,920
Those are the underlying skills

80
00:03:07,920 --> 00:03:09,920
that we use to assemble character

81
00:03:09,920 --> 00:03:12,920
when we're creating stories.

82
00:03:12,920 --> 00:03:13,920
Hmm.

83
00:03:13,920 --> 00:03:15,920
Who's a character in a recent novel

84
00:03:15,920 --> 00:03:17,920
that you still feel like

85
00:03:17,920 --> 00:03:21,920
you have a really intimate relationship with?

86
00:03:21,920 --> 00:03:24,920
Well, I'm very close

87
00:03:24,920 --> 00:03:25,920
to the people in Playground City.

88
00:03:25,920 --> 00:03:28,920
I'm close to the people in Playground still.

89
00:03:28,920 --> 00:03:32,920
And one of the great joys

90
00:03:32,920 --> 00:03:33,920
of being a novelist

91
00:03:33,920 --> 00:03:36,920
is you get up in the morning and you say,

92
00:03:36,920 --> 00:03:38,920
how are they going to surprise me today?

93
00:03:39,920 --> 00:03:41,920
One of the great sadnesses of a novelist

94
00:03:41,920 --> 00:03:43,920
is you have to come to an end

95
00:03:43,920 --> 00:03:45,920
and you have to surrender the manuscript

96
00:03:45,920 --> 00:03:46,920
at some point,

97
00:03:46,920 --> 00:03:48,920
and it becomes this fixed thing.

98
00:03:48,920 --> 00:03:51,920
And yet your heart is still saying,

99
00:03:51,920 --> 00:03:52,920
did I do right by them?

100
00:03:52,920 --> 00:03:54,920
Hmm.

101
00:03:54,920 --> 00:03:57,920
Was there something else that they wanted or needed

102
00:03:57,920 --> 00:03:58,920
that I didn't get to?

103
00:03:58,920 --> 00:04:00,920
So, you know, there's an odd way

104
00:04:00,920 --> 00:04:02,920
in which the character lives on

105
00:04:02,920 --> 00:04:06,920
in your sense of potential or prospect

106
00:04:06,920 --> 00:04:08,920
as you're telling stories.

107
00:04:08,920 --> 00:04:11,920
But, you know, the two central protagonists

108
00:04:11,920 --> 00:04:13,920
in this book,

109
00:04:13,920 --> 00:04:16,920
Todd Keen,

110
00:04:16,920 --> 00:04:20,920
who is a Northside Chicagoan,

111
00:04:20,920 --> 00:04:22,920
white, privileged, wealthy,

112
00:04:22,920 --> 00:04:24,920
and Rafael,

113
00:04:24,920 --> 00:04:26,920
young black guy from the Southside

114
00:04:26,920 --> 00:04:29,920
who's coming from a very different socioeconomic stratum

115
00:04:29,920 --> 00:04:33,920
and whose personal history is very different than Todd's,

116
00:04:33,920 --> 00:04:35,920
in weird ways,

117
00:04:35,920 --> 00:04:38,920
I used them both as alter egos.

118
00:04:38,920 --> 00:04:39,920
Mm.

119
00:04:39,920 --> 00:04:43,920
You know, I used them almost psychoanalytically

120
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:47,920
to get to various things in my personal past

121
00:04:47,920 --> 00:04:49,920
that I wanted still to work through

122
00:04:49,920 --> 00:04:52,920
here at the age of 67.

123
00:04:52,920 --> 00:04:54,920
I put them in colloquial terms.

124
00:04:54,920 --> 00:04:56,920
I put them in a way that I could

125
00:04:56,920 --> 00:04:58,920
say that they were in a collision with each other.

126
00:04:58,920 --> 00:05:00,920
Character leads naturally into drama

127
00:05:00,920 --> 00:05:02,920
in a way that we can talk about.

128
00:05:02,920 --> 00:05:04,920
But I let them collide.

129
00:05:04,920 --> 00:05:06,920
You know, they formed a friendship

130
00:05:06,920 --> 00:05:10,920
that was tight, dedicated,

131
00:05:10,920 --> 00:05:13,920
but also highly competitive

132
00:05:13,920 --> 00:05:17,920
and with a certain degree of wariness and distrust,

133
00:05:17,920 --> 00:05:22,920
a certain degree of ribbing

134
00:05:22,920 --> 00:05:25,920
and crap shoveling,

135
00:05:25,920 --> 00:05:27,920
as males do to each other.

136
00:05:27,920 --> 00:05:29,920
Um...

137
00:05:29,920 --> 00:05:33,920
And the drama that arose out of that

138
00:05:33,920 --> 00:05:36,920
was naturally a kind of extension

139
00:05:36,920 --> 00:05:40,920
of all the unfinished drama of my young life,

140
00:05:40,920 --> 00:05:44,920
you know, from the age of 10 to the age of 30

141
00:05:44,920 --> 00:05:46,920
and beyond.

142
00:05:46,920 --> 00:05:48,920
Character leads into drama.

143
00:05:48,920 --> 00:05:49,920
I think that's true.

144
00:05:49,920 --> 00:05:51,920
I mean, when we think of character,

145
00:05:51,920 --> 00:05:54,920
you know, I used to teach character

146
00:05:54,920 --> 00:05:57,920
using something like the Stanislavski method.

147
00:05:57,920 --> 00:05:59,920
I don't know if you've had writers

148
00:05:59,920 --> 00:06:01,920
who have approached it that way,

149
00:06:01,920 --> 00:06:06,920
but he was a great theoretician of acting

150
00:06:06,920 --> 00:06:08,920
and how to...

151
00:06:08,920 --> 00:06:11,920
An actor prepares, it's one of his works,

152
00:06:11,920 --> 00:06:15,920
and, you know, how to inhabit a role

153
00:06:15,920 --> 00:06:17,920
of someone on stage who isn't you.

154
00:06:17,920 --> 00:06:21,920
And how to locate in the world

155
00:06:21,920 --> 00:06:23,920
that role

156
00:06:23,920 --> 00:06:28,920
something in the core inner values of that character

157
00:06:28,920 --> 00:06:31,920
that you yourself can identify with.

158
00:06:31,920 --> 00:06:32,920
Mm.

159
00:06:32,920 --> 00:06:34,920
Knowing full well that you don't...

160
00:06:34,920 --> 00:06:36,920
You are not that person.

161
00:06:36,920 --> 00:06:38,920
You do not belong to that world.

162
00:06:38,920 --> 00:06:41,920
But somehow abstracting it enough

163
00:06:41,920 --> 00:06:45,920
so that you can use your own inner experiences

164
00:06:45,920 --> 00:06:47,920
and core inner values

165
00:06:47,920 --> 00:06:50,920
to inhabit and participate in that other character's...

166
00:06:50,920 --> 00:06:53,920
And of course, what an actor does to inhabit a role

167
00:06:53,920 --> 00:06:56,920
is not that different than what a novelist has to do

168
00:06:56,920 --> 00:06:58,920
to create a character.

169
00:06:58,920 --> 00:07:00,920
So, make this concrete for me.

170
00:07:00,920 --> 00:07:03,920
I don't know why I was thinking of Nemo.

171
00:07:03,920 --> 00:07:07,920
And Nemo is, you know, this little kid fish,

172
00:07:07,920 --> 00:07:09,920
and Nemo wants to get out... Right.

173
00:07:09,920 --> 00:07:11,920
...and go do things, go explore the ocean.

174
00:07:11,920 --> 00:07:14,920
And then you have, what's his name, Marlin, the dad?

175
00:07:14,920 --> 00:07:15,920
Marlin, I think, is his name.

176
00:07:15,920 --> 00:07:18,920
Marlin is this super overprotective dad. Right.

177
00:07:18,920 --> 00:07:19,920
And Marlin has done very early on,

178
00:07:19,920 --> 00:07:20,920
and he's done very early in the movie,

179
00:07:20,920 --> 00:07:22,920
he's like, are you sure you want to go to school today?

180
00:07:22,920 --> 00:07:24,920
Because it's the first day of school.

181
00:07:24,920 --> 00:07:26,920
And he's trying to say, stay home, stay home,

182
00:07:26,920 --> 00:07:32,920
because he is this just gripping on the life of his young son.

183
00:07:32,920 --> 00:07:35,920
So, if we take that, tell me about what you're saying

184
00:07:35,920 --> 00:07:37,920
through the prism of those characters.

185
00:07:37,920 --> 00:07:39,920
So, I used to do this.

186
00:07:39,920 --> 00:07:43,920
And this also derives from the Stanislavski method,

187
00:07:43,920 --> 00:07:46,920
but I used to teach characterization as an onion.

188
00:07:46,920 --> 00:07:48,920
So, you have...

189
00:07:49,920 --> 00:07:51,920
On the outside of a person,

190
00:07:51,920 --> 00:07:53,920
I don't necessarily mean the outside

191
00:07:53,920 --> 00:07:55,920
in the way that they perform themselves in the world,

192
00:07:55,920 --> 00:07:58,920
because that's always a delicate and elaborate dance,

193
00:07:58,920 --> 00:08:02,920
hiding a lot of things, displaying a lot of things,

194
00:08:02,920 --> 00:08:05,920
you know, negotiating a lot of things.

195
00:08:05,920 --> 00:08:10,920
But in terms of the psychic economy of that character,

196
00:08:10,920 --> 00:08:14,920
in the outer shell are the traits.

197
00:08:14,920 --> 00:08:17,920
And he's got a green shirt on,

198
00:08:17,920 --> 00:08:19,920
it's got a little insignia,

199
00:08:19,920 --> 00:08:21,920
a nice haircut,

200
00:08:21,920 --> 00:08:23,920
you know, he holds one hand...

201
00:08:23,920 --> 00:08:25,920
You know, all the things that you can use

202
00:08:25,920 --> 00:08:29,920
to make a character visual and visible to the reader.

203
00:08:29,920 --> 00:08:34,920
But those traits come from somewhere, right?

204
00:08:34,920 --> 00:08:40,920
They derive from things that are farther in the interior of the onion.

205
00:08:40,920 --> 00:08:45,920
And beneath that level of surface,

206
00:08:45,920 --> 00:08:49,920
you know, physical traits or behavioral traits,

207
00:08:49,920 --> 00:08:52,920
you might have mannerisms.

208
00:08:52,920 --> 00:08:59,920
Like, he likes to unnerve people.

209
00:08:59,920 --> 00:09:02,920
Whenever somebody says something,

210
00:09:02,920 --> 00:09:03,920
instead of saying,

211
00:09:03,920 --> 00:09:05,920
no, right, he'll say,

212
00:09:05,920 --> 00:09:06,920
what do you mean by that?

213
00:09:06,920 --> 00:09:12,920
And that mannerism of challenging or subverting

214
00:09:12,920 --> 00:09:13,920
or undercutting somebody...

215
00:09:13,920 --> 00:09:17,920
Man, there's a sensitivity of awareness

216
00:09:17,920 --> 00:09:19,920
that you must have.

217
00:09:19,920 --> 00:09:21,920
You must really have to have.

218
00:09:21,920 --> 00:09:23,920
And is that something that you're cultivating?

219
00:09:23,920 --> 00:09:25,920
I think it can be cultivated,

220
00:09:25,920 --> 00:09:28,920
because I don't think I ever came by any of this naturally.

221
00:09:28,920 --> 00:09:30,920
You know, if you're talking about,

222
00:09:30,920 --> 00:09:34,920
you know, various distribution curves of people,

223
00:09:34,920 --> 00:09:37,920
some of whom have very high emotional intelligence,

224
00:09:37,920 --> 00:09:41,920
are immediately aligned with reading other people.

225
00:09:41,920 --> 00:09:42,920
And then other people,

226
00:09:42,920 --> 00:09:45,920
you know, the cliché is the other end of the spectrum

227
00:09:45,920 --> 00:09:47,920
are people who do better with machines

228
00:09:47,920 --> 00:09:49,920
or mathematical concepts.

229
00:09:49,920 --> 00:09:53,920
I'm probably on that side of that spectrum.

230
00:09:53,920 --> 00:09:56,920
But I do think we can all,

231
00:09:56,920 --> 00:09:58,920
we are all conversant,

232
00:09:58,920 --> 00:10:00,920
you know, to some degree,

233
00:10:00,920 --> 00:10:04,920
with finding, you know, equivalents and analogs

234
00:10:04,920 --> 00:10:06,920
across the ways of knowing the world.

235
00:10:06,920 --> 00:10:10,920
So the next level inside of mannerisms

236
00:10:10,920 --> 00:10:13,920
would be what I call core inner values.

237
00:10:13,920 --> 00:10:16,920
And I get this again from people

238
00:10:16,920 --> 00:10:19,920
who have done a lot of thinking,

239
00:10:19,920 --> 00:10:23,920
about the creation and inhabiting of characters.

240
00:10:23,920 --> 00:10:25,920
So, but by,

241
00:10:25,920 --> 00:10:30,920
a value has to be something like honesty,

242
00:10:30,920 --> 00:10:33,920
or fidelity,

243
00:10:33,920 --> 00:10:36,920
or perseverance, right?

244
00:10:36,920 --> 00:10:39,920
So we're now seeing,

245
00:10:39,920 --> 00:10:44,920
oh, the reason he holds his hand like that

246
00:10:44,920 --> 00:10:48,920
is he has a manner,

247
00:10:49,920 --> 00:10:52,920
where he wants to set other people at ease.

248
00:10:52,920 --> 00:10:56,920
And underneath that manner

249
00:10:56,920 --> 00:10:58,920
of wanting to set people at ease

250
00:10:58,920 --> 00:11:01,920
is the value of complicity,

251
00:11:01,920 --> 00:11:03,920
or attentiveness,

252
00:11:03,920 --> 00:11:05,920
or, you know, whatever it is.

253
00:11:05,920 --> 00:11:06,920
But you see how multiple values

254
00:11:06,920 --> 00:11:08,920
can drive the same mannerism.

255
00:11:08,920 --> 00:11:09,920
Multiple mannerisms can be,

256
00:11:09,920 --> 00:11:10,920
can lie behind the same trait.

257
00:11:10,920 --> 00:11:11,920
So you have to find,

258
00:11:11,920 --> 00:11:14,920
you have to find ways of making coherence

259
00:11:14,920 --> 00:11:17,920
so that the outward behavior of the person

260
00:11:17,920 --> 00:11:18,920
is both hiding

261
00:11:18,920 --> 00:11:21,920
and revealing things that they need,

262
00:11:21,920 --> 00:11:24,920
things that they want to preserve in the world.

263
00:11:24,920 --> 00:11:27,920
And I would always challenge my students,

264
00:11:27,920 --> 00:11:28,920
I would say,

265
00:11:28,920 --> 00:11:30,920
push them to the wall.

266
00:11:30,920 --> 00:11:31,920
Push them to the wall.

267
00:11:31,920 --> 00:11:32,920
Yeah, so I'd say,

268
00:11:32,920 --> 00:11:35,920
you know this guy is a good guy.

269
00:11:35,920 --> 00:11:38,920
You know he values honesty,

270
00:11:38,920 --> 00:11:41,920
but you know he also values fidelity.

271
00:11:41,920 --> 00:11:44,920
Now, put him in a place

272
00:11:44,920 --> 00:11:46,920
where he cannot have both.

273
00:11:46,920 --> 00:11:47,920
Mm-hmm.

274
00:11:47,920 --> 00:11:48,920
So now you've got to make a choice.

275
00:11:48,920 --> 00:11:49,920
Yes.

276
00:11:49,920 --> 00:11:51,920
What is your core inner value?

277
00:11:51,920 --> 00:11:53,920
And what one will fall by the wayside

278
00:11:53,920 --> 00:11:54,920
when push comes to shove

279
00:11:54,920 --> 00:11:56,920
because you've got to choose?

280
00:11:56,920 --> 00:11:57,920
Right.

281
00:11:57,920 --> 00:11:59,920
I mean, the scenarios are obvious.

282
00:11:59,920 --> 00:12:04,920
You know, your friend has just done something wrong.

283
00:12:04,920 --> 00:12:06,920
Do you go to him and say,

284
00:12:06,920 --> 00:12:09,920
look, man, you've got to pay for that.

285
00:12:09,920 --> 00:12:11,920
You have to own up to that.

286
00:12:11,920 --> 00:12:13,920
Or do you say, I'm a good, faithful friend.

287
00:12:13,920 --> 00:12:15,920
I, you know, I'm just going to do what I want.

288
00:12:15,920 --> 00:12:16,920
I'm going to do what I want.

289
00:12:16,920 --> 00:12:18,920
You know, I'm just going to support this guy.

290
00:12:18,920 --> 00:12:19,920
I'll be with you no matter what.

291
00:12:19,920 --> 00:12:20,920
Yeah.

292
00:12:20,920 --> 00:12:21,920
That's a story.

293
00:12:21,920 --> 00:12:23,920
In fact, that's 10,000 stories.

294
00:12:23,920 --> 00:12:24,920
Yeah.

295
00:12:24,920 --> 00:12:25,920
Right?

296
00:12:25,920 --> 00:12:27,920
So what I'm hearing you say is,

297
00:12:27,920 --> 00:12:29,920
you take these two values,

298
00:12:29,920 --> 00:12:30,920
and you take them to the extreme.

299
00:12:30,920 --> 00:12:33,920
You almost make somebody fight those two values internally.

300
00:12:33,920 --> 00:12:34,920
Yeah.

301
00:12:34,920 --> 00:12:35,920
What would it take?

302
00:12:35,920 --> 00:12:38,920
What situation or challenge would it take

303
00:12:38,920 --> 00:12:42,920
to force that person to have to jump ship from one

304
00:12:42,920 --> 00:12:44,920
and embrace the other?

305
00:12:44,920 --> 00:12:45,920
Now, that's drama.

306
00:12:45,920 --> 00:12:46,920
Yeah.

307
00:12:46,920 --> 00:12:49,920
And that's a very specific kind of drama.

308
00:12:49,920 --> 00:12:52,920
It's interior drama.

309
00:12:52,920 --> 00:12:55,920
And in the hierarchy of drama that I learned way back

310
00:12:55,920 --> 00:12:58,920
in grade school, back in the day,

311
00:12:58,920 --> 00:13:00,920
using very sexist language, that would be called

312
00:13:00,920 --> 00:13:03,920
a man versus himself.

313
00:13:03,920 --> 00:13:04,920
OK?

314
00:13:04,920 --> 00:13:10,920
And that fundamental kind of interior instability,

315
00:13:10,920 --> 00:13:15,920
you know, when I go out into the tumult of the world,

316
00:13:15,920 --> 00:13:18,920
my sense of self has to readjust.

317
00:13:18,920 --> 00:13:22,920
My sense of what I think was most important to me has to change.

318
00:13:22,920 --> 00:13:23,920
Mm-hmm.

319
00:13:23,920 --> 00:13:26,920
That's the classic psychological novel.

320
00:13:26,920 --> 00:13:30,920
How do we cope with the differences in the dramas inside our own head?

321
00:13:30,920 --> 00:13:33,920
Because look, you know, I don't want to have to choose

322
00:13:33,920 --> 00:13:35,920
between honesty and fidelity.

323
00:13:35,920 --> 00:13:37,920
I want to have both.

324
00:13:37,920 --> 00:13:39,920
Well, life doesn't always let you have both.

325
00:13:39,920 --> 00:13:40,920
Right.

326
00:13:40,920 --> 00:13:42,920
And that's where the drama of being alive is.

327
00:13:42,920 --> 00:13:44,920
Can you live with yourself if you have to do something?

328
00:13:44,920 --> 00:13:47,920
If you have to do something that you ordinarily would hate to do,

329
00:13:47,920 --> 00:13:50,920
but the circumstance makes it necessary for you?

330
00:13:50,920 --> 00:13:51,920
Yes.

331
00:13:51,920 --> 00:13:57,920
Now, I'm doing that, and David's doing that too.

332
00:13:57,920 --> 00:14:01,920
And here we are sitting in the same room, right?

333
00:14:01,920 --> 00:14:05,920
And you need something from me, and I need something from you.

334
00:14:05,920 --> 00:14:06,920
Right?

335
00:14:06,920 --> 00:14:10,920
You have core inner value that's very different than my core inner value.

336
00:14:10,920 --> 00:14:13,920
So November's coming up.

337
00:14:13,920 --> 00:14:19,920
Let's say for point of argument that my core inner value is equality,

338
00:14:19,920 --> 00:14:22,920
and your core inner value is freedom.

339
00:14:22,920 --> 00:14:24,920
And now we have to go to the election box,

340
00:14:24,920 --> 00:14:27,920
and we have to vote for one candidate.

341
00:14:27,920 --> 00:14:28,920
Mm-hmm.

342
00:14:28,920 --> 00:14:30,920
I can't believe what you're voting for.

343
00:14:30,920 --> 00:14:31,920
You see what I'm saying?

344
00:14:31,920 --> 00:14:33,920
Now we have interpersonal drama.

345
00:14:33,920 --> 00:14:36,920
Now we have the sociological novel or the political novel

346
00:14:36,920 --> 00:14:41,920
where I can make you completely sympathetic to the reader.

347
00:14:41,920 --> 00:14:42,920
Mm-hmm.

348
00:14:42,920 --> 00:14:43,920
And...

349
00:14:43,920 --> 00:14:46,920
I can make myself completely sympathetic to the reader.

350
00:14:46,920 --> 00:14:50,920
But now the reader's watching two people collide

351
00:14:50,920 --> 00:14:55,920
in a way where they, the reader, has to say who's right.

352
00:14:55,920 --> 00:14:58,920
The author doesn't necessarily have to say who's right,

353
00:14:58,920 --> 00:15:00,920
but the reader has to say,

354
00:15:00,920 --> 00:15:01,920
if that were me...

355
00:15:01,920 --> 00:15:02,920
Which would I do?

356
00:15:02,920 --> 00:15:04,920
How would I jump?

357
00:15:04,920 --> 00:15:05,920
I got to jump.

358
00:15:05,920 --> 00:15:06,920
Got to jump.

359
00:15:06,920 --> 00:15:08,920
Got to put a vote in the ballot box.

360
00:15:08,920 --> 00:15:10,920
So you got person against themselves,

361
00:15:10,920 --> 00:15:11,920
person against person.

362
00:15:11,920 --> 00:15:12,920
Right.

363
00:15:12,920 --> 00:15:13,860
Is that it?

364
00:15:13,920 --> 00:15:14,920
No.

365
00:15:14,920 --> 00:15:16,920
There's a third level of drama,

366
00:15:16,920 --> 00:15:19,920
which is that human beings

367
00:15:19,920 --> 00:15:23,920
want to have a certain story in the world.

368
00:15:23,920 --> 00:15:25,920
They want to have a project.

369
00:15:25,920 --> 00:15:26,920
They want to have...

370
00:15:26,920 --> 00:15:28,920
They have a conception

371
00:15:28,920 --> 00:15:31,920
of what a good life is

372
00:15:31,920 --> 00:15:36,920
and how we can best go about doing that on this Earth.

373
00:15:36,920 --> 00:15:39,920
The rest of the world,

374
00:15:39,920 --> 00:15:42,920
and it is a very large rest of the world,

375
00:15:42,920 --> 00:15:48,920
a very big, comprehensive and interconnected living planet,

376
00:15:48,920 --> 00:15:53,920
might be hostile to that idea of what it is that we want most.

377
00:15:53,920 --> 00:15:55,920
It might be at best indifferent to it,

378
00:15:55,920 --> 00:15:57,920
or it might be very sympathetic to it.

379
00:15:57,920 --> 00:16:02,920
But there is a battle between humans in the aggregate

380
00:16:02,920 --> 00:16:06,920
and a world of which humans are only a very tiny part.

381
00:16:06,920 --> 00:16:07,920
Mm-hmm.

382
00:16:07,920 --> 00:16:10,920
And that level you would call man against the elements

383
00:16:10,920 --> 00:16:11,920
or man against the universe.

384
00:16:11,920 --> 00:16:15,920
Or man against life.

385
00:16:15,920 --> 00:16:21,920
So you have the psychological component of all stories.

386
00:16:21,920 --> 00:16:25,920
You have the sociological or political element of all stories.

387
00:16:25,920 --> 00:16:27,920
But you also have this environmental

388
00:16:27,920 --> 00:16:30,920
or metaphysical element to all stories.

389
00:16:30,920 --> 00:16:31,920
Mm-hmm.

390
00:16:31,920 --> 00:16:33,920
Now, if I...

391
00:16:33,920 --> 00:16:39,920
You know, when I was reading everything I could get my hands on

392
00:16:39,920 --> 00:16:41,920
and deciding that I was going to give my life

393
00:16:41,920 --> 00:16:46,920
to this craft and this work,

394
00:16:46,920 --> 00:16:51,920
and I was reading especially contemporary novels

395
00:16:51,920 --> 00:16:54,920
in the 80s and in the 90s

396
00:16:54,920 --> 00:16:57,920
and even into the early 2000s,

397
00:16:57,920 --> 00:17:00,920
there were a lot, a lot of novels

398
00:17:00,920 --> 00:17:04,920
that were exceptionally good at psychology

399
00:17:04,920 --> 00:17:09,920
and very, very good at sociology and politics,

400
00:17:09,920 --> 00:17:14,920
but almost never ventured beyond the human world.

401
00:17:14,920 --> 00:17:15,920
Mm.

402
00:17:15,920 --> 00:17:18,920
Almost never introduced the idea

403
00:17:18,920 --> 00:17:22,920
that what we want out of our lives on this planet

404
00:17:22,920 --> 00:17:25,920
might not be commensurate with what the planet wants.

405
00:17:25,920 --> 00:17:29,920
So the whole third kind of drama

406
00:17:29,920 --> 00:17:32,920
was disappearing from literary fiction.

407
00:17:32,920 --> 00:17:38,920
We were getting extremely able and capable

408
00:17:38,920 --> 00:17:41,920
in our ability to tell stories about humans,

409
00:17:41,920 --> 00:17:46,920
but only as if humans were autonomous

410
00:17:46,920 --> 00:17:49,920
and inter... independent from everything else.

411
00:17:49,920 --> 00:17:50,920
Right.

412
00:17:50,920 --> 00:17:54,920
And it seemed to me that can't be right.

413
00:17:54,920 --> 00:17:58,920
And I started to look at the history of literary fiction

414
00:17:58,920 --> 00:18:00,920
and then the history of world literature

415
00:18:00,920 --> 00:18:03,920
even before the novel.

416
00:18:03,920 --> 00:18:07,920
And I was looking for that third kind of story.

417
00:18:07,920 --> 00:18:10,920
And I saw, I mean, you can think of great examples of it, right?

418
00:18:10,920 --> 00:18:13,920
I mean, if I said, tell me a book about man versus nature

419
00:18:13,920 --> 00:18:15,920
or man versus, you know, the gods

420
00:18:15,920 --> 00:18:17,920
or man versus the elements.

421
00:18:17,920 --> 00:18:19,920
Greek mythology, a bunch.

422
00:18:19,920 --> 00:18:20,920
All of it.

423
00:18:20,920 --> 00:18:23,920
Almost all mythological traditions.

424
00:18:23,920 --> 00:18:25,920
Now, bring it up to the present.

425
00:18:25,920 --> 00:18:26,920
What do you think, you know,

426
00:18:26,920 --> 00:18:30,920
what's the latest kind of great book in the American canon

427
00:18:30,920 --> 00:18:32,920
that you think really concerned,

428
00:18:32,920 --> 00:18:35,920
took seriously the idea of man versus nature?

429
00:18:35,920 --> 00:18:36,920
I have no idea.

430
00:18:36,920 --> 00:18:37,920
I don't know.

431
00:18:37,920 --> 00:18:38,920
You gotta go back a ways.

432
00:18:38,920 --> 00:18:39,920
Yeah.

433
00:18:39,920 --> 00:18:41,920
And you gotta go back to Moby Dick

434
00:18:41,920 --> 00:18:43,920
or you gotta go back to the frontier,

435
00:18:43,920 --> 00:18:44,920
American frontier novel.

436
00:18:44,920 --> 00:18:45,920
It's been that long, huh?

437
00:18:45,920 --> 00:18:47,920
Well, I mean, there are always exceptions.

438
00:18:47,920 --> 00:18:48,920
Sure.

439
00:18:48,920 --> 00:18:49,920
Right?

440
00:18:49,920 --> 00:18:50,920
But there's a dip.

441
00:18:50,920 --> 00:18:53,920
There's a real dip where, you know,

442
00:18:53,920 --> 00:18:57,920
from 1851 when Moby Dick's published

443
00:18:57,920 --> 00:19:02,920
to, you know, 1914,

444
00:19:02,920 --> 00:19:04,920
the start of the First World War,

445
00:19:04,920 --> 00:19:06,920
you see a trend for sure

446
00:19:06,920 --> 00:19:09,920
where that novel starts to seem quaint,

447
00:19:09,920 --> 00:19:12,920
like the battle against nature.

448
00:19:12,920 --> 00:19:14,920
You know, it's like Jack London, you know,

449
00:19:14,920 --> 00:19:16,920
it's well, you know,

450
00:19:16,920 --> 00:19:19,920
why doesn't it seem literary to us?

451
00:19:19,920 --> 00:19:24,920
Because I think the practitioners of storytelling

452
00:19:24,920 --> 00:19:27,920
were under the mistaken impression

453
00:19:27,920 --> 00:19:30,920
that man had defeated nature,

454
00:19:30,920 --> 00:19:33,920
that human beings had won that drama.

455
00:19:33,920 --> 00:19:35,920
And now we could invoke that drama,

456
00:19:35,920 --> 00:19:38,920
we could invoke that drama kind of nostalgically,

457
00:19:38,920 --> 00:19:40,920
but it wasn't a real issue in our lives anymore.

458
00:19:40,920 --> 00:19:43,920
Our technologies had gotten so powerful

459
00:19:43,920 --> 00:19:47,920
that it didn't seem like questions

460
00:19:47,920 --> 00:19:50,920
that we had to ask anymore.

461
00:19:50,920 --> 00:19:53,920
Now, what happens in the last couple decades

462
00:19:53,920 --> 00:19:55,920
is there's a growing awareness

463
00:19:55,920 --> 00:19:57,920
that we didn't win that war.

464
00:19:57,920 --> 00:19:59,920
In fact, we're losing that war.

465
00:19:59,920 --> 00:20:01,920
And we're in bad shape,

466
00:20:01,920 --> 00:20:02,920
not just on the climate front,

467
00:20:02,920 --> 00:20:04,920
but on the species extinction front.

468
00:20:04,920 --> 00:20:05,920
Yeah.

469
00:20:05,920 --> 00:20:08,920
And now that drama comes flooding back

470
00:20:08,920 --> 00:20:09,920
into the literary novel.

471
00:20:09,920 --> 00:20:13,920
It never left the novel of science fiction.

472
00:20:13,920 --> 00:20:17,920
It never left fantasy.

473
00:20:17,920 --> 00:20:21,920
I mean, fantasy is man against nature, right?

474
00:20:21,920 --> 00:20:24,920
It's telling that those things became second class,

475
00:20:24,920 --> 00:20:28,920
those genres became subordinate genres

476
00:20:28,920 --> 00:20:30,920
in the eyes of the people who wanted

477
00:20:30,920 --> 00:20:34,920
to be practitioners of literary fiction, right?

478
00:20:34,920 --> 00:20:40,920
But now, where it would have been strange for me in 1980

479
00:20:40,920 --> 00:20:43,920
to pick up a new literary fiction

480
00:20:43,920 --> 00:20:46,920
that had a lot of non-human elements in it,

481
00:20:46,920 --> 00:20:48,920
non-human agents or non-human concerns

482
00:20:48,920 --> 00:20:50,920
or taking place seriously

483
00:20:50,920 --> 00:20:53,920
or taking other kinds of creatures seriously,

484
00:20:53,920 --> 00:20:55,920
now it's rare to pick one up

485
00:20:55,920 --> 00:20:57,920
that isn't addressing this question

486
00:20:57,920 --> 00:20:59,920
of how are we going to stay here much longer.

487
00:20:59,920 --> 00:21:02,920
As you were thinking about the overstory

488
00:21:02,920 --> 00:21:04,920
and learning about trees

489
00:21:04,920 --> 00:21:07,920
and developing a kind of empathy for trees, right?

490
00:21:07,920 --> 00:21:09,920
I think of how big is the distance

491
00:21:09,920 --> 00:21:11,920
between you as the person

492
00:21:11,920 --> 00:21:15,920
and the, dare I say, consciousness of a tree.

493
00:21:15,920 --> 00:21:18,920
It almost felt like your mind

494
00:21:18,920 --> 00:21:22,920
and the consciousness of trees fused together.

495
00:21:22,920 --> 00:21:24,920
And I know that in your research process,

496
00:21:24,920 --> 00:21:27,920
you just spent a lot of time in the woods.

497
00:21:27,920 --> 00:21:29,920
And I would love to hear about that research,

498
00:21:29,920 --> 00:21:31,920
but even more fundamentally,

499
00:21:31,920 --> 00:21:33,920
how you developed that kind of empathy

500
00:21:33,920 --> 00:21:37,920
for trees and turning up the dial

501
00:21:37,920 --> 00:21:40,920
of just how real they were to you.

502
00:21:40,920 --> 00:21:45,920
I found that in old literature.

503
00:21:45,920 --> 00:21:51,920
You mentioned mythology, indigenous stories.

504
00:21:51,920 --> 00:21:54,920
And of course, I found it also

505
00:21:54,920 --> 00:22:02,920
by recovering my own earliest sense of narrative.

506
00:22:03,920 --> 00:22:09,920
So, you know, I've often said that young children

507
00:22:09,920 --> 00:22:12,920
are animists, you know,

508
00:22:12,920 --> 00:22:16,920
or, you know, they have,

509
00:22:16,920 --> 00:22:21,920
they take seriously these magical creatures around us.

510
00:22:21,920 --> 00:22:24,920
You know, I just looked at some crows flying past

511
00:22:24,920 --> 00:22:27,920
and looking at these marvelous locusts out of the window,

512
00:22:27,920 --> 00:22:30,920
you know, and a child could look at that

513
00:22:30,920 --> 00:22:33,920
and know that that creature,

514
00:22:33,920 --> 00:22:36,920
that creature is alive in really profound ways

515
00:22:36,920 --> 00:22:38,920
that adults now stop taking seriously.

516
00:22:38,920 --> 00:22:40,920
No, it's just wood. It's just wood.

517
00:22:40,920 --> 00:22:41,920
Right.

518
00:22:41,920 --> 00:22:45,920
So to go back to my own childhood pantheism

519
00:22:45,920 --> 00:22:48,920
and to go back to the stories

520
00:22:48,920 --> 00:22:51,920
that underwrite oral literature

521
00:22:51,920 --> 00:22:54,920
that know that you can't talk about human beings,

522
00:22:54,920 --> 00:22:56,920
you can't understand human beings,

523
00:22:56,920 --> 00:22:59,920
except in conversation with the neighbors,

524
00:22:59,920 --> 00:23:01,920
you know, with understanding them

525
00:23:01,920 --> 00:23:03,920
in the full context of who we're not.

526
00:23:03,920 --> 00:23:06,920
You know, and our fascination with the nonhuman world

527
00:23:06,920 --> 00:23:08,920
is that somehow we can see qualities

528
00:23:08,920 --> 00:23:11,920
in these other ways of being in the world

529
00:23:11,920 --> 00:23:13,920
that resonate with our own values.

530
00:23:13,920 --> 00:23:15,920
So to look at the nonhuman world

531
00:23:15,920 --> 00:23:17,920
is also to understand interior drama.

532
00:23:17,920 --> 00:23:18,920
Mm-hmm.

533
00:23:18,920 --> 00:23:21,920
So it's not just a question of giving trees voice.

534
00:23:21,920 --> 00:23:23,920
It's a question of remembering those voices

535
00:23:23,920 --> 00:23:27,920
inside human beings that were suppressed

536
00:23:27,920 --> 00:23:29,920
by this cultural colonialism that said,

537
00:23:29,920 --> 00:23:31,920
no, no, no, don't, you know,

538
00:23:31,920 --> 00:23:33,920
pay no attention to the world behind you.

539
00:23:33,920 --> 00:23:34,920
It's behind the curtain.

540
00:23:34,920 --> 00:23:36,920
It's just us.

541
00:23:36,920 --> 00:23:40,920
So to recover, to recover that amazement

542
00:23:40,920 --> 00:23:43,920
wasn't a separate thing than, you know,

543
00:23:43,920 --> 00:23:45,920
than to go deep into character.

544
00:23:45,920 --> 00:23:47,920
For me, for the creation of that book,

545
00:23:47,920 --> 00:23:49,920
they became the same enterprise.

546
00:23:49,920 --> 00:23:51,920
Well, what's wild about the overstory

547
00:23:51,920 --> 00:23:55,920
is how articulate you are about trees.

548
00:23:55,920 --> 00:23:57,920
